@@ -0,0 +1,328 @@
+# UR W1 plan: the busy check reads only a running pass
+
+Initiative: unattended-rounds
+Round: ur-ws
+Pass: W1
+
+Design: the build design's section "Pass W1" in
+`~/Projects/initiatives/.claude/worktrees/unattended-rounds/unattended-rounds/specs/2026-11-11-unattended-rounds-build-design.md`
+(lines 231 to 238), approved as Approval Queue item `20261111T175022-2144654`. Its acceptance list
+is fixed. Class `runner`. Worktree `~/.dotfiles/.claude/worktrees/ur-w1` on branch `ur-w1`, push
+target `glw907/workstation`, PR against `main`. Executor `dotfiles-ur-w1` (Sonnet at `medium`)
+under `conductor-dotfiles`. The build design estimates 45 minutes. This plan's path is 50, and a
+path over 90 would have parked.
+
+## Pre-flight
+
+Geoff has no step in this pass: no sign-in, secret, dialog, setting, or permission rule.
+
+Permissions checked: the planner read all 57 rows of `~/.local/state/claude-denials.jsonl`
+(2026-11-01 to 2026-11-11, the whole log, inside the 30-day window) against each task's commands.
+Classes found: unexplained classifier 10, Self-Modification 10, Interfere With Workloads 9, Merge
+Without Review 3, Auto-Mode Bypass 3, Unverifiable Deletion Scope 2, Sensitive-Source Provenance 2,
+Security Weaken 2, Irreversible Local Destruction 2, Git Destructive 2, Create Public Surface 2,
+Classifier unavailable 2, and one each of Shared Scratch Sweep, Secret-Store Writes, Safety Bypass
+Flag, Production Deploy, Out-of-Place Publication, DNS/Domain/Cert, Code from External, and Bot
+Check Circumvention. No row refuses an edit under `bin/.local/bin/` or `tests/`, the test run, the
+gate, a push of a pass branch, `gh pr create`, `gh pr edit`, `gh pr ready`, `gitleaks git`, or
+`claude-tooling-sync verify`. "Refusals" below names the alternative for each refused class a
+task could come near.
+
+Reviewed: one `standards:plan-reviewer` read on 2026-11-11 (Opus 5.5 at `medium`). It found
+nothing that changes the design. Its 14 findings are folded (see "Review").
+
+Evidence: the "Mechanisms" table (every row a probe the planner ran on 2026-11-11, or a quoted
+source line) and the three recorded searches under "Searches".
+
+## What changes
+
+`claude-worktree-busy <directory>` keeps its contract: exit 0 and no output when free, exit 1 and
+one line when busy, exit 2 on a usage error. Its three checks still run in the same order. Two of
+them get narrower.
+
+The session check skips a row whose `state` is `done`, `stopped`, or `failed`. Every other row
+whose cwd is in the path stays a hit, including a row with no `state` key, which is how an
+interactive session's row looks (M1). The check keeps reading `claude agents --json` with no
+`--all`.
+
+The process check skips two command lines, read as an argument list split on NUL bytes:
+
+- The Claude daemon: the first argument's basename is `claude`, and the next two are `daemon` and
+  `run`.
+- The queue's server: the first argument's basename is `node`, and the second ends with
+  `/approval-queue/build`. Later arguments are allowed, since Q1 keeps the command line
+  beginning `node <path>/approval-queue/build`.
+
+Every other process with the path in its command line stays a hit. That includes another `node`
+process and a `claude` process that isn't `daemon run`.
+
+Out of scope: the hit messages, the edit check, the path-matching pattern at line 35, and every
+caller. No `docs/STATUS.md` line changes, because the only line there that names the check is
+line 22, inside the conductor's round-report block.
+
+## Tasks
+
+The three tasks touch the same two files, so they run in sequence. No pair.
+
+**T1. A finished session row is not a hit (10 min, `runner`).**
+Files: `tests/claude-worktree-busy.test.sh`, `bin/.local/bin/claude-worktree-busy`.
+
+Outcome: the session check follows "What changes". The module docstring's sentence on the
+session check says which rows count.
+
+Test, written first. The stub `claude` takes its row's `state` from an environment variable that
+defaults to `working`, and can print a row with no `state` and no `id` key (the interactive
+shape, M1). The two cases at lines 58 and 59 keep their exit 1 on the default state. Add these
+cases, each with the stub row's cwd set to the repo:
+
+| Label | Stub row | Exit |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `finished session row` | `state` `done` | 0 |
+| `stopped session row` | `state` `stopped` | 0 |
+| `failed session row` | `state` `failed` | 0 |
+| `session row with no state` | no `state`, no `id` | 1 |
+
+Every exit-1 case in the file, old and new, also asserts which check fired, by the line's
+opening words (`busy: process`, `busy: claude session`, or `busy: uncommitted file`). Without
+that, a case can pass on the wrong check.
+
+Acceptance check: run `bash tests/claude-worktree-busy.test.sh` before touching the script. It
+must exit 1 and print a `FAIL:` line for each of the three exit-0 labels above. Keep that
+output for the PR. After the script change the same command prints `OK: claude-worktree-busy`
+and exits 0. Then run the fast lane as "Gate runs" says, exit 0, and commit both files by path.
+
+**T2. The daemon and the queue's server are not hits (15 min, `runner`).**
+Files: the same two.
+
+Outcome: the process check follows "What changes". The module docstring names the two skipped
+command lines.
+
+Test, written first. Build each fixture process the way M7, M8, and M13 show, with `exec -a`
+setting the first argument, so no real `node` or `claude` binary runs. Give every fixture
+process a short sleep, end it as the existing sleeper cases do (the test's own `kill` of the pid
+it started, plus the `EXIT` trap), and never leave one running past its case.
+
+| Label | Fixture's argument list | Checked path | Exit |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `daemon process holds path` | `<root>/bin/claude`, `daemon`, `run`, `--origin`, `transient`, `--spawned-by`, a JSON string holding `"cwd":"<repo>"` | `<repo>` | 0 |
+| `queue server holds path` | `node`, `<root>/approval-queue/approval-queue/build` | `<root>/approval-queue` | 0 |
+| `queue server and a finished row` | the server process above, plus a stub row with `state` `done` and cwd `<root>/approval-queue` | `<root>/approval-queue` | 0 |
+| `other node process holds path` | `node`, `-c`, a sleep, `<repo>/other.js` (M13; no such file needs to exist) | `<repo>` | 1, `busy: process` |
+| `node process with a longer file name` | `node`, `<root>/approval-queue/approval-queue/build.old` | `<root>/approval-queue` | 1, `busy: process` |
+| `claude process that is not daemon run` | `claude`, `daemon`, `status`, `<repo>` (M13) | `<repo>` | 1, `busy: process` |
+
+Four traps. The first is a race, and the other three come from the edit check running last.
+
+- A fixture's command line is the test's own until its `exec` lands. A check that runs in that
+  window sees no path, so an exit-0 case passes with a wrong script and an exit-1 case fails
+  with a right one. Add one helper to the test that reads `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` every 0.05
+  seconds, for 2 seconds at most, until it starts with the first argument the case expects, and
+  fails the case if it never does. Call it before every `check` that has a fixture process, the
+  three existing sleeper cases included.
+- `<root>/approval-queue` is a fixture git repo. A fresh untracked file in it is an edit hit, so
+  commit its `approval-queue/build` and `approval-queue/build.old` files by path before the
+  cases run. Otherwise the two server cases fail on `busy: uncommitted file` with a correct
+  script.
+- Put every new case that checks `<repo>` before the existing line 57. From line 62 to line 64
+  `<repo>/new.txt` is a fresh uncommitted file, so an exit-0 case placed there fails on the edit
+  check with a correct script.
+- Never run `git add -A` or `git add .` in a fixture repo. It would commit `new.txt`, and the
+  case `uncommitted file aged past 60 minutes` would then pass without testing anything.
+
+Acceptance check: before touching the script, `bash tests/claude-worktree-busy.test.sh` exits 1
+with a `FAIL:` line for each of the three exit-0 labels above, and no `FAIL:` line for the three
+exit-1 labels. Keep that output for the PR. After the script change it prints
+`OK: claude-worktree-busy` and exits 0 on three runs in a row. Then run the fast lane as "Gate
+runs" says, exit 0, and commit both files by path.
+
+**T3. Close (25 min, `runner`).**
+Files: `BACKLOG.md`, and the PR body.
+
+1. File the one backlog item this plan's "Found, not built" section describes, with
+   `standards:log-issue`, at priority medium. Right before the commit, run `git fetch origin`
+   and read the highest `**#N**` in `git show origin/main:BACKLOG.md`: the new item takes the
+   next number, since other sessions add items on `main` and a clash would conflict at the
+   merge. Commit `BACKLOG.md` by path.
+2. Run `git push -u origin ur-w1`, then the full gate on the head commit as "Gate runs" says.
+3. Run `gitleaks git --no-banner --redact --log-opts="origin/main..HEAD" .` from the worktree.
+   It exits 0.
+4. Run `claude-tooling-sync verify` from the worktree. It reads the machine's live `~/.claude`
+   state, and on 2026-11-11 it exits 1 on a line this branch can't cause (M14). When every `x`
+   line names something outside this branch's three files, paste the output into `## Decisions`
+   and go on. Never run `claude-tooling-sync apply` and never edit a setting to clear it. An `x`
+   line that names one of this branch's files is this branch's to fix.
+5. Read usage with `claude -p "/usage" --model haiku < /dev/null`. Then dispatch one
+   `standards:diff-reviewer` at whole-branch scope with `model: "fable"` and effort `high`
+   (`model: "opus"` when the Fable line reads 95 percent or more or can't be read, named in
+   `## Decisions`). The dispatch names this plan, the four items under "Acceptance" with the
+   evidence for each, and `standards:python-conventions` as the checklist for the script, and
+   tells the reviewer to check each item against its evidence. Act on the verdict as the round
+   rules' "Executor" item 5 says. After a fix chain, push and run the full gate again.
+6. Open the PR against `main` in the form the round rules' "Executor" items 2 to 4 set, with a
+   `## Brief` first, and mark it ready once the review line reads `accept`. Its `## Decisions`
+   records the planner's calls: the three finished states, the two argument-list shapes, no
+   `docs/STATUS.md` edit, and `BACKLOG.md` as a third file (`pass-core` "Close" files a
+   follow-up there). Its `## Planning misses` carries the `[design-gap]` and `[stale-fact]`
+   lines this plan names, and one `[estimate]` line if the path passed 50 minutes.
+
+Acceptance check: `gh pr view --json isDraft,baseRefName,body` shows `isDraft` false, base
+`main`, and a body holding the `Round:`, `Pass:`, `Review:`, and `Calls:` lines; `git status
+--short` in the worktree prints nothing; and `git rev-list --count origin/ur-w1..HEAD` prints 0
+after a fetch.
+
+## Gate runs
+
+Run each gate command bare, as its own Bash call from the worktree, with the Bash tool's
+`timeout: 600000`. The default timeout is 120 seconds and the full gate took 185 (M10), so a
+call without the longer timeout loses the run.
+
+- Fast lane, after T1 and after T2: `bash scripts/gate.sh --scope-changed`. For the two task
+  files it runs 11 legs (M10).
+- Full gate, at close: `bash scripts/gate.sh`.
+
+The wrapper waits on a lane lock that every session on the machine shares. On exit 75 run the
+same command again, and keep doing so until it prints a `gate exit:` line. Never pipe a gate
+into `tail` or `head`, and never poll a log. The new shell code in the test meets `shellcheck -S
+warning`, and a new docstring in the script meets ruff's `D` rules, `D401` (an imperative first
+line) among them.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+The four items are the build design's, verbatim. The evidence for each is the test command, the
+label of each case that proves it with its line number in the test file, the kept `FAIL:` lines
+from the run before the script changed, and the `OK` line from the head commit.
+
+1. A session row whose `state` is `done` is not a hit.
+   T1. `bash tests/claude-worktree-busy.test.sh`, case `finished session row`.
+2. A `claude daemon run` process and the queue's server (`node <path>/approval-queue/build`) are not hits.
+   T2. The same command, cases `daemon process holds path` and `queue server holds path`.
+3. A running `--bg` session in the path and an uncommitted file edited in the last hour are hits, as today. So is any other process with the path in its command line, a `node` process that isn't the queue's server among them.
+   T1 and T2. The same command, cases `session cwd under path` and `session cwd reaches the path
+   through a symlink` (stub row `state` `working`), `fresh uncommitted file`, `process holds
+   path`, `other node process holds path`, `node process with a longer file name`, and `claude
+   process that is not daemon run`. These cases pass before and after, so their evidence is the
+   `OK` line and the message assertions.
+4. With a fixture holding the queue's server process and one finished session row, the check exits 0 for the approval-queue path.
+   T2. The same command, case `queue server and a finished row`.
+
+## Mechanisms
+
+Probes ran on host pavilion on 2026-11-11 with claude 2.1.322, against the script at `1864585`.
+`<tmp>` is a temporary directory and `<pid>` a process id.
+
+| # | Fact | Probe or source, with output |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| M1 | A live background row has `state` `working`. A finished one has `done`. An interactive row has no `state` and no `id`. | `claude agents --json`, rows grouped by kind, state, and status: 3 `background` `done` `idle`; 5 `background` `working` `busy`; 1 `interactive`, no `state`, `idle`, no `id`. `busy` is a `status` value, never a `state`. |
